Why not have the Demon Lord claim the soul of the hapless adventurer
who summoned him and return to Hell with his prize.  Possibly taking
any nifty toys the adventurer might have been carrying with him when
he goes.  It removes the excessive threat (and reward), and as a bonus
it makes more "story" sense than having him peaceful.

The meditation idea doesn't actually remove the out-of-depth threat,
as the demon could be next to the stairs and the player's pet could
wake him before the player has time to stop it.  You may need to add
boulder-blocked stairs to make this plausible, but it could be
constructed with a no-E demon.

A completely different suggestion: you could tie E's effectiveness to
either Dlvl (or max Dlvl) or XP-lvl rather than/in addition to monster
level.  This would let E still help against early encountered
out-of-depth monsters, but its effectiveness should fade as you get
further into the game and should have more resources to deal with
problems.  I like Dlvl best, I think; it ties nicely into a general
feeling of "shallower is safer" for the game.  (Another option, I
suppose, would be to make it another "influence of the gods doesn't
reach here" cutoff for Gehennom)

The point is, rather than your "tougher monsters ignore the word", a
different design principle would be "tougher characters don't need the
word".  Find some appropriate time to remove/reduce the effectiveness
of the crutch and the big monsters become a threat because of the
combination of what they can do and where they're found.


